----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[K: e8-d7, K: e8-d8, P: g7-g6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "I need to analyze this critical position where I'm playing as Black.\n\n## Current Position Analysis:\n\nLooking at the board:\n- My King (KBF0) is on e8, unmoved\n- I have a White Knight (NWF1) deep in my position on d8 - this is a serious threat\n- My pieces are mostly undeveloped\n- White has active pieces including knights on c3 and c2, bishops on e3 and h5\n\n## Valid Moves Available:\n1. **K: e8-d7** - King moves to d7\n2. **K: e8-d8** - King captures the White Knight on d8\n3. **P: g7-g6** - Pawn moves to g6\n\n## Evaluation:\n\n**Move 1: K: e8-d7**\n- Moves king away but leaves the powerful White Knight on d8\n- The knight on d8 can fork, control key squares, and severely restrict my position\n- King on d7 is somewhat exposed\n- Does NOT solve the immediate problem\n\n**Move 2: K: e8-d8**\n- Captures the dangerous White Knight\n- Removes a major threat from my position\n- King ends up on d8, which is relatively safe\n- Material gain (capturing a knight worth ~3 points)\n- Improves my position significantly\n\n**Move 3: P: g7-g6**\n- Attacks the White Bishop on h5\n- But ignores the critical threat of the Knight on d8\n- The knight could deliver devastating tactics next turn\n- Passive and doesn't address the main issue\n\n## Reason:\nThe White Knight on d8 is a massive positional and tactical threat deep in my territory. I must eliminate it immediately. Capturing with K: e8-d8 removes this dangerous piece, gains material, and my king will be reasonably safe on